<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/maven-v4_0_0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
    <groupId>com.amazon.aws</groupId>
    <artifactId>pix</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0</version>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <name>AWS PIX (Brazilian Instant Payment System)</name>

    <properties>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <!--
          The BYTECODE TARGET is 11. The RUNTIME is JDK 17. Those are different things and
          conflating them is a mistake this comment exists to prevent - I made it once already.
        
          CloudHSM Client SDK 5 requires OpenJDK 17, 21 or 25 to RUN; 5.17.1 was the last release
          supporting 11. That says nothing about what bytecode we emit, and measured, the SDK 5
          provider jar is itself major version 52 - Java 8 bytecode - so compiling against it
          imposes no target at all.
        
          What DOES impose a target is Quarkus 1.7. With this set to 17 our classes become major
          61 and its augmentation step fails outright:
        
            Build step ClassTransformingBuildStep#handleClassTransformation threw an exception:
            IllegalArgumentException: Unsupported class file major version 61
        
          Measured. Note it did NOT fail everywhere - cloudhsm/proxy packaged fine, because its
          classes never reach that transformer, so a 17 target looked healthy in the one module
          that matters while breaking another. That is exactly the shape of a defect that ships.
        
          So: emit 11, build and test on JDK 17, run on JDK 17. Raising this to 17 is gated on
          the Quarkus upgrade (1.7 -> 2.13 -> 3.x), not on anything CloudHSM requires.
        -->
        <java.version>11</java.version>
        <maven.compiler.source>${java.version}</maven.compiler.source>
        <maven.compiler.target>${java.version}</maven.compiler.target>

        <quarkus.version>1.7.0.Final</quarkus.version>
        <camel-quarkus.version>1.0.0</camel-quarkus.version>

        <aws.bom.version>2.13.0</aws.bom.version>
        <!--
          Lombok 1.18.12 CANNOT RUN as an annotation processor on JDK 17 - the build fails to COMPILE
          with IllegalAccessError on JavacProcessingEnvironment, before any test runs. Measured with a
          control: JDK 11 + 1.18.12 compiles, JDK 17 + 1.18.12 does not, JDK 17 + this version does.
          JDK 17 support first appeared in 1.18.22, which is the floor; this version also covers
          JDK 21 and 25, which is where the CloudHSM SDK 5 migration has to go.
        -->
        <lombok.version>1.18.48</lombok.version>

        <junit.version>4.13.1</junit.version>
        <commons-io.version>2.14.0</commons-io.version>

        <!--
          Security overrides. quarkus-bom 1.7.0.Final pulls Netty 4.1.49.Final and
          jackson-databind 2.11.2, both of which carry advisories that matter here:

            Netty / request smuggling, CWE-444, TWICE. 4.1.49 carries CVE-2021-43797
            (netty-codec-http accepts a header name containing a control character, so a
            downstream server can be made to see a request boundary the upstream one did
            not; fixed in 4.1.71.Final). An independent review then caught that pinning
            to 4.1.118 - the first fix attempted here - left CVE-2025-58056 open, which
            is smuggling of the SAME class via chunk-extension parsing and is fixed only
            in 4.1.125.Final. Closing one and leaving its equivalent open is not a fix,
            so the pin now sits past every netty-codec-http advisory currently published,
            verified against the GitHub advisory API rather than by picking a recent
            number. CVE-2025-58057 (netty-codec DoS) is covered by the same move.

            This repository is a proxy forwarding attacker-influenced HTTP/1.1 to the
            BCB, which is precisely the deployment shape those advisories describe, so
            "low severity" ratings understate the relevance here.

            jackson-databind 2.11.2 - the rationale previously given was WRONG and is
            corrected rather than quietly dropped. It cited CVE-2020-25649 as "an XXE
            exposure in a process that also handles signed XML", but that advisory
            enumerates [2.6.0,2.6.7.4), [2.7.0.0,2.9.10.7) and [2.10.0.0,2.10.5.1) - no
            2.11.x range - and its vector is the XML dataformat, while
            jackson-dataformat-xml is not on this classpath at all and the signed-XML
            path uses javax.xml.crypto. The pin is still correct and still worth having:
            it clears the real 2.x databind deserialization CVEs such as
            CVE-2022-42003 / CVE-2022-42004, fixed in 2.13.4.x. The defect was the
            justification, not the remediation.

          netty-tcnative moves with Netty because the two are released as a matched pair;
          the version below is the one netty-parent itself declares.

          These BOM imports are deliberately declared BEFORE quarkus-bom: for imported
          BOMs Maven takes the FIRST declaration that manages an artifact, so putting
          them after quarkus-bom would leave the vulnerable versions in place and the
          override would silently do nothing. Verified with `dependency:list`, not by
          assuming - see README-CloudHSM.md.
        -->
        <netty.version>4.1.138.Final</netty.version>
        <netty-tcnative.version>2.0.84.Final</netty-tcnative.version>
        <!--
          CVE-2026-59888 affects the 2.15.x range and is fixed in 2.18 and later, so the previous
          2.15.4 pin is not defensible for a payment system. 2.21 is the current LTS line rather than
          the newest 2.x, which is the right trade for a system that must pass homologação and then run
          for years. Jackson 3 is released and deliberately not taken: a major version with API changes
          this repository has no need for.
        -->
        <jackson.version>2.21.2</jackson.version>

        <!--
          CloudHSM Client SDK 5 JCE provider, pinned as one unit. All four values move together.

          WHY SDK 5 AT ALL: SDK 3 cannot talk to hsm2m.medium, and hsm2m.medium is the only HSM type
          that can still be created - hsm1.medium reached end of support on 2026-03-31. SDK 3 was not
          a preference to abandon, it was unreachable hardware.

          The jar is NOT on Maven Central. cloudhsm/jce5 downloads this rpm, verifies the digest,
          unpacks it and installs the jar as com.amazonaws:cloudhsm-jce:${cloudhsm.sdk5.version} in
          the local repository. That module's comment explains why the artifact is provided-scope and
          never bundled; the short version is that the jar is architecture-specific AND code-signed.

          The Dockerfile installs the SAME version into the image. Those two must not drift, so CI
          compares them - see the "cloudhsm - compile" job.

          RECURRING OBLIGATION, not a one-off: from SDK 5.17 AWS supports up to three prior minor
          versions and one year from the release date, and disables download links for older ones.
          Pinning by digest turns that into a TIMED failure - the hash stays correct, the object
          disappears, and the build reports a missing file rather than an expired version. The
          operational calendar in README-CloudHSM.md exists for this.

          Supported JDKs for this provider are OpenJDK 17, 21 and 25 only. 5.17.1 was the last release
          to support OpenJDK 11, which is why java.version above cannot go back to 11.
        -->
        <cloudhsm.sdk5.version>5.18.0</cloudhsm.sdk5.version>
        <cloudhsm.sdk5.rpm.version>5.18.0-1</cloudhsm.sdk5.rpm.version>
        <!-- Filename suffix: lowercase, as it appears inside the rpm name. -->
        <cloudhsm.sdk5.rpm.platform>amzn2023</cloudhsm.sdk5.rpm.platform>
        <!-- S3 path segment: CAPITALISED. Measured - the lowercase path returns HTTP 403. -->
        <cloudhsm.sdk5.rpm.pathsegment>Amzn2023</cloudhsm.sdk5.rpm.pathsegment>
        <cloudhsm.sdk5.rpm.sha256>2ee2076bea49b970e1b9f6a0edee11dc9cbd139ef11a2687e4e6f54c9cc3ef66</cloudhsm.sdk5.rpm.sha256>
    </properties>

    <modules>
        <module>core</module>
        <module>cloudhsm</module>
        <module>kms</module>
        <module>test</module>
    </modules>

    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>

            <!--
              MUST stay above quarkus-bom: the first imported BOM that manages an
              artifact wins, so moving these down re-introduces the CVEs.
            -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>io.netty</groupId>
                <artifactId>netty-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${netty.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>

            <!--
              netty-tcnative has no BOM artifact, so it is managed directly. 2.0.70.Final
              is the version Netty 4.1.118.Final itself declares in netty-parent, and the
              two are released as a matched pair - moving Netty without moving tcnative is
              how the OpenSSL provider ends up loading against the wrong JNI ABI. The
              classifier stays linux-x86_64-fedora, which is the one that carries the
              Amazon Linux-compatible native build.
            -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>io.netty</groupId>
                <artifactId>netty-tcnative</artifactId>
                <version>${netty-tcnative.version}</version>
                <classifier>linux-x86_64-fedora</classifier>
            </dependency>
            <!--
                The aarch64 classifier needs its OWN managed entry: dependencyManagement matches on
                groupId:artifactId:type:CLASSIFIER, so the entry above does not cover it and the build
                fails with "version is missing". That is how this entry came to exist, not foresight.
                
                The two classifier names are deliberately ASYMMETRIC. Measured against Maven Central at
                2.0.84.Final: linux-x86_64, linux-x86_64-fedora and linux-aarch_64-fedora are published,
                and a plain linux-aarch_64 IS NOT. Do not "correct" the asymmetry - it stops resolving.
            -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>io.netty</groupId>
                <artifactId>netty-tcnative</artifactId>
                <version>${netty-tcnative.version}</version>
                <classifier>linux-aarch_64-fedora</classifier>
            </dependency>

            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson</groupId>
                <artifactId>jackson-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${jackson.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>

            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.amazon.aws</groupId>
                <artifactId>pix-core</artifactId>
                <version>1.0.0</version>
            </dependency>

            <dependency>
                <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
                <artifactId>quarkus-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${quarkus.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>

            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.apache.camel.quarkus</groupId>
                <artifactId>camel-quarkus-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${camel-quarkus.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>

            <dependency>
                <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
                <artifactId>bom</artifactId>
                <version>${aws.bom.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>

            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
                <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
                <version>${lombok.version}</version>
                <scope>provided</scope>
            </dependency>

            <dependency>
                <groupId>junit</groupId>
                <artifactId>junit</artifactId>
                <version>${junit.version}</version>
                <scope>test</scope>
            </dependency>

            <dependency>
                <groupId>commons-io</groupId>
                <artifactId>commons-io</artifactId>
                <version>${commons-io.version}</version>
            </dependency>

        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <build>
        <pluginManagement>
            <plugins>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>3.6.1</version>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>3.1.1</version>
                    <configuration>
                    </configuration>
                    <executions>
                        <execution>
                            <phase>package</phase>
                            <goals>
                                <goal>shade</goal>
                            </goals>
                        </execution>
                    </executions>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
                    <artifactId>quarkus-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${quarkus.version}</version>
                    <executions>
                        <execution>
                            <goals>
                                <goal>build</goal>
                            </goals>
                        </execution>
                    </executions>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.jboss.jandex</groupId>
                    <artifactId>jandex-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>1.0.7</version>
                    <executions>
                        <execution>
                            <id>make-index</id>
                            <goals>
                                <goal>jandex</goal>
                            </goals>
                        </execution>
                    </executions>
                </plugin>
            </plugins>
        </pluginManagement>
    </build>
</project>
